      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the standard implications of a force majeure clause in a Singapore commercial lease agreement, specifically regarding how it handles sudden, prolonged business interruptions that prevent a tenant from operating?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A force majeure clause in a Singapore commercial lease agreement is designed to excuse parties from fulfilling contractual obligations when an unforeseen, overwhelming event occurs. It can be invoked when events like natural disasters, war, or pandemics make performance impossible or commercially impracticable. For a tenant facing prolonged business interruptions, this clause typically acts as a shield, but the specifics matter as the contract will dictate whether it suspends the lease, allows for rent abatement, or permits early termination. It's crucial to examine the precise wording of the clause to understand the scope of the excused event and the resulting relief.",
      "source": "target",
