    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm looking at a specific case where the interruption wasn't a natural disaster but more like a series of chaotic, unpredictable administrative failures and local infrastructure collapses that made it impossible to get staff to the site. would a court generally view that kind of 'unforeseeable chaos' as falling under the umbrella of force majeure, or is that too vague?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A court would likely view \"chaotic, unpredictable administrative failures and local infrastructure collapses\" as a much tougher fit for a standard force majeure clause, which usually requires a more catastrophic, external event. These types of issues often fall into the realm of *frustration* or breach of implied duty, rather than the classic definition of force majeure. If the clause is narrowly written, it will likely only cover things like earthquakes or government shutdowns, not routine, albeit severe, logistical breakdowns. You need to check if the clause has a broad \"catch-all\" phrase that explicitly covers governmental action or widespread civil disorder.",
      "source": "target",
