    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, i see. so if we can't rely on force majeure because of that vagueness, what are the typical legal thresholds for proving \"frustration of purpose\" in a Singapore court? like, at what point does the disruption become so extreme that the entire lease agreement is considered commercially dead?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proving \"frustration of purpose\" in Singapore is a high bar, requiring that the entire commercial purpose of the lease has been rendered radically different from what was originally intended. It's not enough that the operation is difficult or unprofitable; the event must destroy the *entire* commercial foundation of the agreement. The disruption must be so severe that it's not just a temporary setback, but a fundamental, unforeseen change that makes performance legally or physically impossible. If the tenant can still perform the lease, even at a great cost, frustration is unlikely to succeed.",
      "source": "target",
